WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:09.140
So I recently just completed
a short film about computers,

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.443
and I'm very excited to be able
to premiere it right now at TED.

00:00:12.443 --> 00:00:13.477
(Cheers)

00:00:13.511 --> 00:00:14.945
Let's take a look. Thank you.

00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:16.614
(Soothing music)

00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:31.862
(Screaming)

00:00:32.463 --> 00:00:34.198
(Laughter)

00:00:35.132 --> 00:00:36.400
Did that scare you?

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:38.102
(Laughter)

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:42.306
I think that's because computers
actually are kind of scary, aren't they?

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:45.276
Hi, my name is Alan Resnick,

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:48.112
and if you don’t know me,
I’m a bit of a “tech-head.”

00:00:48.112 --> 00:00:49.380
What does that mean?

00:00:49.413 --> 00:00:52.049
It means an average day for me
is spent surfing the web,

00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:55.186
checking my emails
and just clicking on links.

00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:57.088
That's sort of me in a nutshell.

00:00:57.121 --> 00:00:58.522
And when I'm not doing that,

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:02.927
I tour the country, and I teach people
how to use computers.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.729
And the number one thing
I've noticed all over

00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:08.799
is that people are afraid of computers.

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.401
They're scared.

00:01:10.434 --> 00:01:14.105
And don't get me started
on the internet, you guys.

00:01:14.138 --> 00:01:16.006
Has anyone here ever been online?

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.174
(Laughter)

00:01:17.208 --> 00:01:19.410
Two, three hands.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.045
You guys gotta get online.

00:01:21.078 --> 00:01:22.646
It's incre --

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.948
I'm telling you,

00:01:23.948 --> 00:01:26.350
there's some crazy stuff online, you guys.

00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:29.320
I don't have time
to get into it right now,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.890
but they have photos and videos
of people online,

00:01:32.923 --> 00:01:37.294
and they're performing a kind of love
that I am not comfortable with.

00:01:37.328 --> 00:01:38.596
OK?

00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:39.797
(Laughter)

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:41.098
And they have your data.

00:01:42.032 --> 00:01:44.468
They do. These sick people have your data.

00:01:44.502 --> 00:01:46.504
Are you comfortable with that? I'm not.

00:01:47.338 --> 00:01:50.274
But I have a question for you guys,
and it is a real question.

00:01:50.307 --> 00:01:52.676
How do you use your computer?

00:01:52.676 --> 00:01:54.378
How do you guys use your computers?

00:01:54.378 --> 00:01:56.247
Just curious.

00:01:56.247 --> 00:01:57.515
Can we get her a mic?

00:01:57.515 --> 00:02:00.117
How do you use your computer?

00:02:00.117 --> 00:02:01.986
Audience Member: Well, I usually use

00:02:01.986 --> 00:02:02.987
my computer for work.

00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:05.723
So emails and, you know,
sending messages to folks.

00:02:05.756 --> 00:02:07.925
Alan Resnick: No, stop. OK. Shut up. No.

00:02:07.958 --> 00:02:09.760
OK. This is wrong.

00:02:09.793 --> 00:02:10.895
(Laughter)

00:02:10.928 --> 00:02:15.633
This is incorrect. I hate to say this,
but you're using your computer wrong.

00:02:15.666 --> 00:02:18.435
You know, not "use."
I don't love that word "use."

00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:20.738
We don't "use" computers, do we?

00:02:20.738 --> 00:02:24.508
No. I think of computers
a little like grandparents, actually.

00:02:24.542 --> 00:02:26.510
You wouldn't use your grandmother, right?

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.511
(Laughter)

00:02:27.545 --> 00:02:30.447
But your grandmother can
make your life easier

00:02:30.447 --> 00:02:32.716
if you use her properly.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.486
So what is the proper way
to use a computer?

00:02:35.486 --> 00:02:38.088
Tonight I'm going to share with you
my one simple trick

00:02:38.122 --> 00:02:40.224
that's going to make
your life a lot easier,

00:02:40.257 --> 00:02:43.260
it's going to protect your data
and it's going to calm you down.

00:02:43.294 --> 00:02:44.695
OK, here it is.

00:02:44.695 --> 00:02:45.963
(Clears throat)

00:02:46.864 --> 00:02:48.132
(Whispers) Desktops.

00:02:49.366 --> 00:02:51.569
Desktops.

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:52.836
What is he saying?

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.138
(Whispers) Desktop.

00:02:54.171 --> 00:02:57.675
I can't hear him. What are you saying?
I'm saying save it to your desktop!

00:02:57.708 --> 00:03:00.110
It's as simple as that.
Save it to your desktop.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.812
OK? Yeah, we can clap for that.

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:04.148
That's pretty cool. That is pretty cool.

00:03:04.181 --> 00:03:06.383
You're going,
"Alan, it can't be that easy."

00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:07.384
It is.

00:03:07.384 --> 00:03:09.153
But before
we can talk about desktops,

00:03:09.153 --> 00:03:11.956
we have to talk about something else.

00:03:11.956 --> 00:03:14.491
Look at this. Check this out.
This is pretty cool.

00:03:15.059 --> 00:03:17.261
Look at that. You see this?

00:03:17.261 --> 00:03:18.329
My skin. Look at --

00:03:18.329 --> 00:03:19.363
Whoa!

00:03:19.363 --> 00:03:21.198
My skin is vibrating.

00:03:21.198 --> 00:03:24.101
But it's vibrating --
it's very hard to tell.

00:03:24.134 --> 00:03:26.337
It's hard to see.
Here. This is vibrating, too.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.073
But I think you'll be able to see this.
Can you see that?

00:03:29.073 --> 00:03:30.341
Light vibrates too.

00:03:30.374 --> 00:03:32.643
Are you -- I need a response.

00:03:32.676 --> 00:03:34.278
Can you see that? You can? OK.

00:03:34.278 --> 00:03:35.613
(Laughter)

00:03:35.613 --> 00:03:38.215
That's because of the eye-brain system.

00:03:38.249 --> 00:03:40.784
The visible spectrum.
Rods and cones. Rods and cones.

00:03:40.784 --> 00:03:42.052
Rods and cones.

00:03:42.086 --> 00:03:44.188
But my skin is vibrating, too.

00:03:44.221 --> 00:03:47.491
But it vibrates at a rate
that you can't see or perceive,

00:03:47.491 --> 00:03:51.228
but dogs and pets can actually hear.

00:03:51.262 --> 00:03:53.364
They hear our skin.

00:03:53.364 --> 00:03:55.633
So I know you like to touch
a little dog's head.

00:03:55.666 --> 00:03:56.900
(Imitates barking)

00:03:56.934 --> 00:04:00.738
But that passive action is actually
causing them extreme inner ear pain,

00:04:00.738 --> 00:04:02.239
so you shouldn't touch dogs.

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:03.941
(Laughter)

00:04:03.974 --> 00:04:05.509
But here's what you didn't know.

00:04:07.611 --> 00:04:09.113
My computer vibrates too.

00:04:09.113 --> 00:04:10.948
Did you know that? Computers vibrate.

00:04:10.948 --> 00:04:15.052
And they happen to vibrate
at the exact opposite rate as human skin.

00:04:15.085 --> 00:04:16.387
That's amazing.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.057
So when you're working with a computer,
those vibrations are phasing.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:24.028
And when you touch a computer,
you have a vibration rate of zero.

00:04:24.028 --> 00:04:26.397
There is only one moment
in every person's life

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.399
when they have a vibration rate of zero,

00:04:28.432 --> 00:04:33.070
and that is on their fifth birthday,
when they blow out the candle.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:35.005
So you go on, "Alan, you convinced me."

00:04:35.039 --> 00:04:37.408
If working with computers
feels even half as good

00:04:37.408 --> 00:04:38.976
as a little boy's birthday,

00:04:39.009 --> 00:04:40.544
then sign me up.

00:04:40.577 --> 00:04:44.982
The glove is off and we're ready
to change our lives forever.

00:04:45.015 --> 00:04:46.283
But how?

00:04:46.283 --> 00:04:47.551
(Whispers) Desktops.

00:04:47.584 --> 00:04:49.019
I already told you, desktops.

00:04:49.053 --> 00:04:51.221
Save it to your desktop!
I already said that.

00:04:51.255 --> 00:04:52.823
Save it to your desktop. OK.

00:04:52.823 --> 00:04:53.924
(Laughter)

00:04:53.957 --> 00:04:55.392
I think you're ready.

00:04:57.027 --> 00:04:58.762
Do you guys want to see my desktop?

00:04:58.762 --> 00:05:01.365
Audience: Yes. Show me your desktop.

00:05:01.365 --> 00:05:02.366
AR: I can’t hear you.

00:05:02.366 --> 00:05:04.134
Do you guys want to see my desktop?

00:05:04.134 --> 00:05:06.036
Come on! OK, let's do it!

00:05:06.036 --> 00:05:08.439
Here we go. Let's get it booted up.

00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:11.008
Here it is. It's my desktop.

00:05:11.008 --> 00:05:12.443
Whoa!

00:05:12.443 --> 00:05:13.711
That's amazing.

00:05:13.744 --> 00:05:16.647
You're going, "Alan, I could never
have something like that."

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.216
If you follow my simple rule,
save it to your desktop,

00:05:19.249 --> 00:05:21.552
you're not only going to have
an awesome desktop,

00:05:21.585 --> 00:05:24.088
you're actually going to have
a wicked, awesome life.

00:05:24.121 --> 00:05:25.923
Now check this out. This is cool.

00:05:25.956 --> 00:05:28.525
The desktop --

00:05:28.559 --> 00:05:33.097
is a little like
my desk's top surface, right?

00:05:33.097 --> 00:05:35.032
So I have file folders on the computer.

00:05:35.032 --> 00:05:38.068
And guess what? I got them here too.

00:05:38.068 --> 00:05:40.904
What kind of things can you save
to your desktop? Anything.

00:05:40.904 --> 00:05:45.275
You can have photos, emails, PDFs.

00:05:45.309 --> 00:05:46.643
Those are like packets.

00:05:46.677 --> 00:05:50.214
You can have movies, video games,

00:05:50.247 --> 00:05:54.318
key codes, MP3s, zip disks.

00:05:54.318 --> 00:05:57.821
These are all useful things
that you can save to your desktop.

00:05:57.821 --> 00:05:59.990
So it's sort of
an organizational technique.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.658
So I'll give you an example.

00:06:01.658 --> 00:06:05.396
Let's say your dad emails you
some pics of the lawn.

00:06:05.429 --> 00:06:07.898
All you have to do is ...

00:06:07.931 --> 00:06:12.002
OK. Yeah, you got to just
save them to the desktop.

00:06:13.337 --> 00:06:15.606
Just save those right to the desktop.

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:16.974
(Laughter)

00:06:17.007 --> 00:06:20.644
And after you do that, you're going
to want to print out a copy for yourself,

00:06:20.677 --> 00:06:22.880
which you're going to place
on your desk's top.

00:06:22.913 --> 00:06:25.616
Can I get those photos?
Where the heck are those photos?

00:06:25.616 --> 00:06:26.884
Oh, thank you so much.

00:06:26.917 --> 00:06:28.919
So --

00:06:28.952 --> 00:06:30.421
This is cool.

00:06:30.454 --> 00:06:32.523
So now you always know
where everything is.

00:06:32.556 --> 00:06:34.358
You're not going to lose anything.

00:06:34.391 --> 00:06:37.728
I'll give you another example.
I'm just going to pick a file at random.

00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:39.897
Let's just pick a random file.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.032
OK? Eeny meeny miny moe.

00:06:42.032 --> 00:06:43.967
Catch a tiger by the t --

00:06:44.001 --> 00:06:45.235
Oh.

00:06:47.004 --> 00:06:49.239
This is a little embarrassing.

00:06:49.239 --> 00:06:51.675
This is actually an old poem I wrote.

00:06:51.708 --> 00:06:53.310
Egg on my face. It says,

00:06:53.343 --> 00:06:57.681
"Why whisper when a yell
can travel just as far?

00:06:57.714 --> 00:07:00.984
A whisper yelled is a whisper heard."

00:07:00.984 --> 00:07:02.586
Hmm.

00:07:02.619 --> 00:07:04.788
(Laughter)

00:07:06.757 --> 00:07:10.928
So where did I save that?
Let's see. Where did I save that?

00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:13.730
It looks like I put that on...

00:07:13.764 --> 00:07:19.269
OK, that's C5, that's the bottom
right corner of my desk.

00:07:19.303 --> 00:07:20.804
(Laughter)

00:07:20.838 --> 00:07:22.072
So it should be here.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:24.808
Oh, Goddamn it!

00:07:25.375 --> 00:07:26.677
Oh! Boom! Here it is!

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.146
Wow! You can clap for that. That's cool!

00:07:29.179 --> 00:07:30.781
This is amazing. This is amazing.

00:07:30.781 --> 00:07:31.882
(Applause)

00:07:31.915 --> 00:07:35.185
Now I'm noticing there's a couple
eagle-eyed viewers in the audience,

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:36.887
and they're looking at my computer

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.523
and they're going,
"Something's up, something's up."

00:07:39.556 --> 00:07:43.594
You're going, "Alan,
where the heck is your toolbar?"

00:07:43.627 --> 00:07:46.263
Or dock. Some people call it dock.
I call it a toolbar.

00:07:46.296 --> 00:07:48.966
"Where's your toolbar?" Well, guess what.

00:07:48.999 --> 00:07:51.768
My computer doesn't have a toolbar.

00:07:51.802 --> 00:07:55.272
Why? I'm not addicted to my toolbar.

00:07:55.305 --> 00:07:58.108
You think America's
got an obesity problem?

00:07:58.141 --> 00:08:01.712
You think we have a fentanyl problem?
Folks, we have a toolbar problem.

00:08:01.745 --> 00:08:03.714
Stop using your toolbar.

00:08:03.747 --> 00:08:06.817
There is room for everything you need
right here on the desktop.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:08.085
(Laughter)

00:08:15.993 --> 00:08:17.261
This is silly.

00:08:18.495 --> 00:08:20.464
This is my --

00:08:20.497 --> 00:08:22.132
My girlfriend,

00:08:22.165 --> 00:08:24.434
my ex-girlfriend, actually,
made this for me.

00:08:24.468 --> 00:08:25.469
(Laughter)

00:08:25.502 --> 00:08:26.737
And, uh...

00:08:27.504 --> 00:08:30.407
I don't know, it's silly,
but I like to look at it.

00:08:30.407 --> 00:08:31.942
It makes me happy to see this,

00:08:31.975 --> 00:08:33.410
so I --

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:34.511
(Sighs)

00:08:34.545 --> 00:08:36.980
I keep it on my desk while I work.

00:08:37.014 --> 00:08:38.248
But, uh --

00:08:39.583 --> 00:08:40.817
Yeah.

00:08:41.618 --> 00:08:44.054
No, no, it's, um --

00:08:44.955 --> 00:08:46.456
It's not in the computer.

00:08:48.292 --> 00:08:51.261
Yet! But literally anything
can be saved to your desktop.

00:08:51.295 --> 00:08:56.033
All you have to do is rig up a camera
around a bucket at 90 degrees.

00:08:56.033 --> 00:08:58.669
You're going to want
to fill the bucket with black water

00:08:58.702 --> 00:09:00.470
and paint your beloved object white.

00:09:00.504 --> 00:09:02.472
And here's what you do. It's really easy.

00:09:02.506 --> 00:09:06.009
You submerge the object in the water
and film yourself as you pull it up,

00:09:06.043 --> 00:09:07.811
recording its topology through time.

00:09:07.844 --> 00:09:10.948
You're going to get the topology
of every axis, axes, axi.

00:09:10.948 --> 00:09:14.351
And once you have the axis data,
all you have to do is boolean the axis.

00:09:14.384 --> 00:09:17.521
You’re going to end up with an OBJ file
that’s super high quality,

00:09:17.554 --> 00:09:20.490
you can render in an infinite quality
of lighting conditions.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.559
And then there's only
one thing left to do.

00:09:22.593 --> 00:09:23.894
What are we going to do?

00:09:23.894 --> 00:09:25.596
(Audience) Save it to your desktop.

00:09:25.596 --> 00:09:27.664
AR: Yes. Oh my God.
Save it to your desktop!

00:09:27.664 --> 00:09:29.833
Yes! Woo! If you do this,

00:09:29.833 --> 00:09:32.636
you will always have the things
you want and need and love,

00:09:32.669 --> 00:09:34.805
and you will know
exactly where to find them.

00:09:34.838 --> 00:09:36.506
You never have to be lonely again.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.609
You will be happy
and people will love you.

00:09:38.642 --> 00:09:42.212
Listen, if you don't have
organizational skills and desktop skills,

00:09:42.245 --> 00:09:45.315
I'm sorry, folks,
you're not going to get that job.

00:09:45.349 --> 00:09:48.385
Your data, it's going to be
leaking on the floor.

00:09:48.418 --> 00:09:52.022
People are going to be slipping.
They're going to be slipping on your data.

00:09:52.055 --> 00:09:53.757
You can say "bye-bye" to your kids.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.360
They're going to be living
on their desktops.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:00.297
Mark my words, in one year's time,
people will literally be,

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.799
blah, blah, blah, blah,
talking to their computers.

00:10:02.799 --> 00:10:05.502
Whoa. In two years,
people are going to be walking around

00:10:05.535 --> 00:10:08.672
with glasses so powerful
they're never going to need to come home.

00:10:08.705 --> 00:10:11.174
And in three years' time,

00:10:11.208 --> 00:10:12.943
computers will have gotten so small

00:10:12.943 --> 00:10:16.546
they will literally fit
on a piece of breakable glass.

00:10:18.448 --> 00:10:20.183
(Groans)

00:10:21.451 --> 00:10:22.519
(Crack)

00:10:22.552 --> 00:10:24.721
Whew! OK. Thank you so much.

00:10:24.755 --> 00:10:26.023
My name is Alan Resnick.

00:10:26.056 --> 00:10:28.392
I really hope that helped.
You've been incredible.

00:10:28.392 --> 00:10:30.894
I love you so much. Good night.
Thank you, thank you.

00:10:30.927 --> 00:10:32.562
Thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:10:32.596 --> 00:10:34.131
(Applause and cheers)

